    "source_icon": "https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url=https://www.facebook.com&client=EDUCATION&size=16&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL",
    "votes": 4
   },
   {
    "answer": "At sea level plain water boils at 212 F. Superheated water can go above 212 F without boiling, but this is an unusual case. Water in a pressurised container, such as a pressure cooker, can also go above 212 F without boiling.",
    "link": "https://www.reddit.com/r/AskCulinary/comments/adg48x/if_i_boil_water_in_a_pot_does_the_water_ever_go/",
    "question": "So just wondering, as I know the stove top goes much higher, but the double pot method would mean that the temperature of the water in the pot could not go above boiling right? Otherwise, the double pot method wouldn't work at higher stove temperatures. Should note this is for in a home, at sea level, in a normal pot with normal filtered water.",
    "source": "Reddit",
    "source_icon": "https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url=https://www.reddit.com&client=EDUCATION&size=16&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL",
    "votes": 6
   },
   {
    "answer": "Water boils at 212°F at standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm or 760 mmHg), which is the boiling point for pure water at sea level. Notes: • Boiling point varies with pressure: at higher altitude (lower pressure) the boiling point is lower; under pressure (e.g., a pressure cooker) it is higher. • Conversion: 212°F corresponds to 100°C and 373.15 K.",
    "link": "https://www.quora.com/What-is-the-boiling-point-of-water-in-Fahrenheit",
    "question": "What is the boiling point of water in Fahrenheit?",
    "source": "Quora",
    "source_icon": "https://encrypted-tbn3.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url=https://www.quora.com&client=EDUCATION&size=16&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL",
    "votes": 0
   },
   {
    "answer": "\"The correct answer is 212°F. The boiling point of a liquid is the temperature at which the vapour pressure of a liquid equals the standard sea-level atmospheric pressure. At sea level, the boiling point of water is 100°C that is equal to 212°F. Celsius to Fahrenheit formula is F= 95 x C + 32 = (95) x 100 + 32 = 180+32 = 212ºF. Measurement °Fahrenheit °Celsius The boiling point of water 212° 100° The freezing point of water 32° 0° Human Body Temperature ~98° (Approx.) ~37°\"",
    "link": "https://testbook.com/question-answer/what-is-boiling-point-of-water-in-fahrenheit--5e58bee3f60d5d6249450eaf",
    "question": "\"What is boiling point of water in Fahrenheit?\"",
    "source": "Testbook",
    "source_icon": "https://encrypted-tbn2.gstatic.com/faviconV2?url=https://testbook.com&client=EDUCATION&size=16&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL",
    "votes": 2307
   },
